import Mathlib

/-!
# The balanced ternary carrier, and what its projections really count

The upstream tranche uses a nine-coordinate balanced-ternary carrier
`{-1, 0, +1}^9` — the flattened `27^3 = 3^9` "interaction" state space — and
records four numbers about it: the carrier has `19683` states, the antipodal
quotient of one ternary pair has `5` classes and of one ternary triple `14`,
and the *aggregate-zero fibre* (the words whose coordinates sum to zero) has
`3139` elements.  Upstream, the last of these is produced by convolving the
coefficient row of `(x⁻¹ + 1 + x)^n` with `[1,1,1]` and reading off the middle
entry; nothing connects that row to the set it is supposed to count, and the
class counts are cardinalities of a hand-written constructor list.

Here they are theorems about the carrier itself:

* `Ternary.card_word` — the carrier of `n` balanced-ternary coordinates has
  `3 ^ n` elements;
* `Ternary.card_sumFibre` — the number of words whose coordinates sum to a
  given `k` *is* the convolution row `Ternary.rowCount`, proved by induction on
  the number of coordinates, so that `Ternary.card_sumFibre_nine_zero` counts
  the zero fibre of the nine-coordinate carrier at exactly `3139` elements;
* `Ternary.sum_rowCount` — the whole row totals `3 ^ n`, again as a statement
  about the words rather than about the list;
* `Ternary.two_mul_card_orbits` — the antipodal quotient of the
  `n`-coordinate carrier satisfies `2 * #classes = 3 ^ n + 1`, from which the
  upstream counts `5`, `14` and `9842` follow (`card_orbits_two`,
  `card_orbits_three`, `card_orbits_nine`).

The general orbit count is an instance of `Ternary.two_mul_card_pairImage`: an
involution with exactly one fixed point on a finite type has `(card + 1) / 2`
orbits.  The antipode has exactly one fixed point because the centre `0` is not
a pole — the upstream `zeroIsNotNegativePole` observation, here doing real work.
-/

